# Draft knowledge entry: eigenpy test_package should fall back to static ELF inspection on OHOS
id: DRAFT-eigenpy-ohos-test-package-loader-fallback
trigger_tool: conan|python|loader
phase: test_package|runtime
error_signature: "dlopen|Permission denied|eigenpy_pywrap|import eigenpy|ELF magic|libpython3.12.so.1.0|Error loading shared library|No module named 'numpy'"

severity: 2
category: eigenpy|ohos|test_package|loader
match_keywords: eigenpy, test_package, dlopen, import eigenpy, eigenpy_pywrap, llvm-readelf, LD_LIBRARY_PATH, libpython3.12, missing numpy

title: eigenpy test_package should add libpython runtime path before OHOS import fallback
root_cause: >
  On OHOS, eigenpy links libeigenpy and eigenpy_pywrap against the selected
  device Python runtime. The package dependency libdirs do not include the
  interpreter's own LIBDIR/LIBPL, so test_version or import eigenpy can fail to
  load libpython3.12.so.1.0 at runtime. Some official CI workers also provide
  Python headers without the runtime NumPy module. After LD_LIBRARY_PATH is
  fixed, the installed Python extension can still be correct at the ELF level
  while Python import fails because loader policy, user-path runtime constraints,
  or missing runtime NumPy block dlopen/import. A consumer test should prefer
  the real Python import path, but when that path is blocked it should verify the
  installed extension statically and report the platform limitation instead of a
  build defect.
affected_scope: >
  eigenpy 3.13.0 OHOS consumer validation and other Python-extension packages
  whose import path may be constrained by HNP loader policy.
verification_cmd: |
  python3 test_import.py
  LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<package-libdirs>:<python-libdir> ./test_version
  llvm-readelf -d <installed eigenpy_pywrap>

rec_priority: 1
rec_approach: add-python-libdir-then-import-first-elf-fallback
rec_description: >
  Locate the selected Python interpreter's LIBDIR/LIBPL, add that libpython
  directory to LD_LIBRARY_PATH together with package dependency libdirs, then
  keep the Python import smoke test as the first validation step. If the loader
  prevents import or the worker lacks runtime NumPy but the extension is a valid
  ELF object, fall back to checking the extension's ELF magic and NEEDED entries
  and record the issue as a platform runtime limitation.
rec_command: |
  LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<package-libdirs>:<python-libdir> ./test_version
  python3 test_import.py
  llvm-readelf -d <eigenpy_pywrap>
rec_auto_fix: false
rec_impl: ohos-test-package-loader-fallback

fallback_priority: 2
fallback_approach: sign-consumer-executable-and-inspect-elf
fallback_description: >
  Sign the test executable before running on OHOS. If Python import is blocked,
  inspect the installed extension statically rather than failing the package for
  a platform loader policy reason.
fallback_command: |
  llvm-readelf -d <installed eigenpy_pywrap>
fallback_auto_fix: false
fallback_impl: ohos-loader-policy-elf-check

forbid_1_pattern: "replace.*Python import.*with only static inspection"
forbid_1_reason: The package still needs a real Python import path when the runtime allows it.
forbid_1_consequence: The consumer test would stop proving the actual Python integration path.

forbid_2_pattern: "LD_LIBRARY_PATH=.*<package-libdirs only>"
forbid_2_reason: eigenpy's OHOS runtime link now needs the selected interpreter's libpython directory too.
forbid_2_consequence: test_version and import eigenpy can fail at runtime with libpython3.12.so.1.0 loader errors.

principles: import-first-validation, static-elf-fallback-for-loader-policy, include-libpython-runtime-dir
source_event: eigenpy/3.13.0 HarmonyOS PC main adaptation after Boost.Python dependency merge
last_updated: 2026-08-29
